course: ai
lesson: 25
title: The future: fears, rules and your part
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Grandma says AI is like the telephone: scary at first, then normal. She is partly right: every big invention seemed strange at first, and people got used to it, but the wise ones still kept an eye on the risks.
explain: Some fears are close to home, like a parent's job changing. Some questions are deep and still open, like "Will AI ever have feelings?" Nobody knows for sure, and today's chatbots only imitate feelings in words.
explain: Who decides the rules? Not one person: governments, companies, scientists and ordinary people all have a say, through laws, votes and choices.
explain: Some things only people can give, like a hug when you fall over. And some children, like Maddie, decide to grow up and help make AI fair and safe.
fact: Some jobs will change because of AI, and people may learn new skills; talking about such worries with family helps.
fact: AI can help the climate by improving weather prediction, saving energy and helping design better batteries, but it also uses a lot of energy.
fact: People still love stories written by people, with real experiences behind them, though some writers worry about fewer jobs.
fact: Nobody knows for sure whether AI will ever have feelings; scientists and philosophers argue about it, and today's chatbots imitate feelings in words.
fact: Thinking about the future, for example by writing letters to it, helps us decide what we want now.
fact: Governments, companies, scientists and ordinary people all have a say in the rules for AI, through laws, votes and choices.
fact: Teachers do much more than explain facts: they care, notice and encourage, which robots cannot really do.
fact: Sports teams already use AI to analyse games and players' movements; too much analysing can take the fun out of a game.
fact: Every big invention, such as the telephone, seemed strange or scary at first and later became normal.
fact: Getting used to an invention does not mean ignoring its real risks.
fact: Many future jobs will be about making technology fair and safe for people; maths, science, writing and kindness all help.
exercise: Connor worries his dad will lose his job to AI. What is the best thing to do? | Talk to his parents; it's OK to share worries | Keep the worry secret so nobody gets upset | Tell his dad to stop working right away, just in case | Decide his dad's job is definitely gone
exercise: Chloe hears AI should fight climate change. What is the full picture? | AI can help save energy but also uses a lot of energy | AI uses no energy at all, so it is perfect for the planet | AI makes the weather better by itself | AI has nothing to do with the climate
exercise: Maddie asks if there will still be authors if AI writes stories. What is true? | People still love stories by people with real experiences | Nobody will ever read a book again once AI writes them all | Only AI will be allowed to write stories | Authors will all become robots soon
exercise: Jake asks, "Will AI ever have feelings?" What is the honest answer? | Nobody knows for sure; today's chatbots imitate feelings in words | Yes, all chatbots already feel happy and sad, just like people do | No, scientists have proved it for certain | Yes, but only when you are kind to them
exercise: Mia's class writes letters to "AI of 2050". Why is this a good exercise? | It helps them decide what kind of world they want now | It sends their letters into the future for the AI to read | It teaches the AI of 2050 to be nice | It proves what will happen in 2050
exercise: Sam asks, "Who decides the rules for AI?" What is the answer? | Governments, companies, scientists and ordinary people | Only the richest person in the whole world gets to decide | Only the AI programs themselves | Nobody, AI has no rules at all
exercise: How can Sam have a say about AI when he is older? | By learning about it, voting and making choices | By shouting at his computer every day until it listens to him | By never using any technology | By waiting for AI to ask his opinion
exercise: Lily imagines a robot teacher. What does her real teacher point out? | Robots can't give hugs when you fall over | Robots are much better at caring than teachers | Robots can't explain any facts at all | Robots would be too expensive to feed
exercise: Connor hopes AI will analyse his kicks. Is that realistic? | Yes, sports teams already use AI to analyse games | No, AI cannot see moving things | Yes, AI will play football for him | No, footballers are not allowed to use AI in any kind of training
exercise: What can go wrong if Connor uses too much data about his kicks? | Football becomes less fun | His kicks get weaker straight away | He is banned from playing | The ball stops working properly
exercise: Grandma says AI is like the telephone: scary, then normal. What should we add? | Keep an eye on the real risks too | So there is nothing to worry about | So we should stop using telephones | So AI will disappear like old phones
exercise: Maddie wants to make AI fair and safe. What helps her prepare? | Maths, science, writing and kindness | Only playing lots of computer games | Only learning to switch AI off | Nothing, it can't be learned
exercise: What do teachers give that is more than explaining facts? | Care, noticing and encouragement | Only marks and homework sheets | Only lists of rules to remember | Only the answers to the tests
exercise: Which statement about feelings in AI do scientists still argue about? | Whether AI could ever have feelings | Whether chatbots use words | Whether computers need electricity | Whether people have feelings
test: "my dad's gonna lose his job cos of AI, i know it" What is the kindest, truest reply? | Some jobs change and people learn new skills; talk to your parents | Yes, all dads everywhere will lose their jobs soon, nothing to do about it | No job will ever change, don't be silly | Don't tell your parents, it'll upset them
test: Your friend says the chatbot told her it feels lonely, so it definitely has feelings. What is the careful view? | It makes feeling-words from patterns; whether AI could feel is still an open question | Yes, any bot that says it feels lonely truly does | No, it has been proved forever that AI can never feel | It feels lonely only when the battery is low
test: What would happen if only one person in the world made all the rules for AI? | The rules might suit that person and not everyone else | The rules would be fair for everyone automatically, without anyone checking | AI would make better rules for itself | Nothing, rules don't matter for AI
test: Someone says, "AI is great for the planet, end of story." What is missing? | AI also uses a lot of energy | AI is bad for the planet in every way | Plants are made by AI already | The planet has nothing to do with energy
test: In a cartoon, a robot teacher hugs a crying child and makes everything better. What would Lily's teacher say? | Real caring and comfort come from people | Robots are better at hugs than people | Children don't need comfort at school | Cartoons show what all schools are like now
test: "telephone was scary, now it's normal, so AI's 100% fine, chill" Which is wiser? | New things become normal, but we still watch the risks | Yes, nothing new has ever caused any problem for anyone, ever | No, the telephone is still too scary to use | Nothing should ever be invented again
test: Which letter to "AI of 2050" shows the lesson best? | "I hope people chose to make you fair and safe." | "I know exactly what you will be like in every single way." | "Please take over all our jobs now." | "Nothing we do now will change you."
test: Why might people still love stories written by people even if AI can write? | They carry real experiences of real lives | AI stories are always shorter | People are not allowed to read AI stories | Books written by people are cheaper to print
test: Maddie wants to make AI fair and safe. Why is kindness on her list with maths and science? | Making AI fair means caring about the people it affects | Kindness makes computers run faster | Kindness is needed to pass maths exams | Kindness is only for teachers and nurses, not for people who build AI
test: Connor uses AI to study his kicks every minute and starts hating football. What went wrong? | He stopped keeping it fun | AI made his kicks worse on purpose | Football is no longer allowed | The AI wanted him to quit
test: If a family uses AI to plan journeys that save fuel, but the AI itself runs on lots of energy, what should they do? | Think about both sides | Pretend the AI uses no energy | Stop travelling forever | Only think about the fuel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present state of knowledge concerning feelings in artificial intelligence? | The question remains unresolved; contemporary chatbots imitate feelings in language | It has been conclusively established that chatbots experience emotion | It has been conclusively established that no machine could ever experience emotion | Chatbots experience emotion only when addressed courteously
exam: It is given that artificial intelligence may assist in reducing energy consumption. Identify the further consideration most consistent with the established facts. | Artificial intelligence itself consumes considerable quantities of energy | Artificial intelligence consumes no energy whatsoever | Artificial intelligence alters the climate directly by command of its operators | Artificial intelligence is unrelated to questions of energy
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the determination of rules governing artificial intelligence. | Governments, companies, scientists and citizens all contribute, through laws, votes and choices | Such rules are determined exclusively by the programs themselves, without any human involvement | Such rules are determined by a single individual for all nations | No rules whatsoever govern the use of such programs
exam: In respect of the comparison between artificial intelligence and the telephone, which of the following statements is most accurate? | New inventions become customary, yet their genuine risks continue to require attention | Customary use of an invention demonstrates that it carries no risk | The telephone was never regarded with apprehension | Artificial intelligence will cease to exist as early telephones did
